---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import { DOCS_ORIGIN } from "../../shared/site-origin";
import { states, statePath } from "../../components/site-data";

const live = states.filter((s) => s.coverage === "live");
const restrictions = states.filter((s) => s.coverage === "restrictions");
const MCP = `${DOCS_ORIGIN}/mcp`;
const cursorConfig = JSON.stringify({ mcpServers: { "burn-ban": { url: MCP } } }, null, 2);
const vscodeConfig = JSON.stringify({ servers: { "burn-ban": { type: "http", url: MCP } } }, null, 2);
const genericConfig = JSON.stringify({ mcpServers: { "burn-ban": { type: "http", url: MCP } } }, null, 2);
const alertPrompt = `Every morning at 7, use the burn-ban tools to check list_active_bans for TX and check_burn_ban for ZIP 78701. Only message me if a county I care about is "ban" or "restricted", and include the official link.`;
const sample = JSON.stringify(
  {
    query: { zip: "74103" },
    answer: "none",
    generated: "2026-11-23T19:09:00Z",
    ambiguous: false,
    approximate: false,
    matchedZip: "74103",
    results: [
      {
        fips: "40143",
        state: "OK",
        county: "Tulsa County",
        status: "none",
        label: "No county or governor burn ban reported",
        since: null,
        expires: null,
        sourceUpdated: null,
        firstSeenAt: "2026-11-23T19:09:00Z",
        statusChangedAt: null,
        checked: "2026-11-23T19:07:00Z",
        sourceName: "Oklahoma Forestry Services",
        sourceUrl: "https://services3.arcgis.com/…/FeatureServer/0",
        officialUrl: "https://experience.arcgis.com/experience/8bdc59b2a54a4383a9a2996fb9407580/",
        url: "/us/ok/tulsa-county/",
      },
    ],
    disclaimer: "Informational only. Always confirm with the official source before burning.",
  },
  null,
  2,
);
---
<Base
  title="Connect your agent to burn ban status · Is There a Burn Ban?"
  description="Connect an agent to published US county burn reports and restrictions through a free MCP server or JSON API."
  path="/developers/"
>
  <main id="content" class="page dev">
    <nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <ol>
        <li><a href="/">Home</a></li>
        <li aria-current="page">Connect your agent</li>
      </ol>
    </nav>
    <h1>Connect your agent</h1>
    <p>
      Connect your assistant to county burn-ban reports and their sources. Once connected, ask <q>Is there a burn ban in Tulsa?</q>
      The server is free and requires no account or key.
    </p>
    <p class="mcp-url"><span>MCP server URL</span> <code>{MCP}</code></p>
    <p class="muted">Setup steps checked against each client's docs on Nov 23, 2026. Menus move; if one doesn't match, look for "custom connector" or "MCP server".</p>

    <h2 id="claude">Claude</h2>
    <p><strong>claude.ai, Claude Desktop, Cowork</strong>: Customize → Connectors → <em>+ Add</em> → <em>Add custom connector</em>. Name it "Burn ban", paste the URL above, and choose <em>No sign in</em>. Free plans allow one custom connector. On Team and Enterprise an owner adds it under Organization settings → Connectors.</p>
    <p><strong>Claude Code</strong>:</p>
    <pre><code>claude mcp add --transport http burn-ban {MCP}</code></pre>
    <p>Add <code>--scope user</code> to use it in every project. Run <code>/mcp</code> to check the connection.</p>

    <h2 id="chatgpt">ChatGPT</h2>
    <p>On the web: Plugins → <em>+</em> → <em>Add custom MCP server</em>. Enter a name and the URL above, set authentication to <em>No authentication</em>, confirm the warning, select <em>Create as a plugin</em>, then install it. Availability depends on your plan and workspace settings.</p>

    <h2 id="editors">Cursor and VS Code</h2>
    <p>Cursor: <code>~/.cursor/mcp.json</code> (all projects) or <code>.cursor/mcp.json</code> (one project).</p>
    <pre><code>{cursorConfig}</code></pre>
    <p>VS Code: <code>.vscode/mcp.json</code>, or run <em>MCP: Add Server</em> and choose HTTP.</p>
    <pre><code>{vscodeConfig}</code></pre>
    <p>Other MCP clients that take JSON (streamable HTTP, stateless, no auth):</p>
    <pre><code>{genericConfig}</code></pre>

    <h2 id="tools">What your agent can do</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><code>check_burn_ban</code> looks up county reports for a ZIP, coordinates, FIPS or incorporated place. Confirm the county when the location is approximate.</li>
      <li><code>list_active_bans</code> lists published bans and restrictions, including scoped orders. Pass a state to narrow the list.</li>
      <li><code>coverage</code> shows the scope, cadence and health of the sources we check.</li>
      <li><code>subscribe_email_alerts</code> requests alerts. When email delivery is enabled, the first email asks you to confirm.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The subscription tool stores a pending signup, so clients may ask you before it runs.</p>

    <h2 id="alerts">Get an alert</h2>
    <p>Use a scheduled lookup or subscribe on the site.</p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>Ask your agent to check on a schedule.</strong> In an assistant that runs scheduled tasks (for example ChatGPT Tasks, or a Claude Code routine via <code>/schedule</code>), use this example prompt.
        <pre><code>{alertPrompt}</code></pre>
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Subscribe on the site.</strong> Open your county page and tap <em>Alert me</em> for push notifications, or have your agent call <code>subscribe_email_alerts</code> with your email. After confirmation we attempt alerts for eligible changes we detect. Scheduled rules and weather signals are excluded.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h2 id="http">Plain HTTP, for any agent or script</h2>
    <p>
      The read API uses JSON with open CORS. Source schedules vary. Add <code>format=text</code> for a plain-language report.
      Follow the response's cache headers and include the publisher and official link when quoting a result.
    </p>
    <pre><code>curl "{DOCS_ORIGIN}/api/v1/status?city=Tulsa&amp;state=OK&amp;format=text"
curl "{DOCS_ORIGIN}/api/v1/status?zip=74103"
curl "{DOCS_ORIGIN}/api/v1/status?lat=36.154&amp;lon=-95.993"
curl "{DOCS_ORIGIN}/api/v1/status?fips=40143"
curl "{DOCS_ORIGIN}/api/v1/bans?state=TX"</code></pre>
    <p>
      <a href="/openapi.json">Read API in OpenAPI</a> · <a href="/llms.txt">llms.txt</a>.
      Every county and city page also links its JSON with <code>&lt;link rel="alternate" type="application/json"&gt;</code> and a <code>Link</code> header.
    </p>

    <h2>Status values</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><code>ban</code> means a reported burn ban. Check which area the order covers.</li>
      <li><code>restricted</code> includes local bans, standing rules and activity-specific limits.</li>
      <li><code>none</code> means the feed reported no temporary county ban within its coverage.</li>
      <li><code>unknown</code> means the county's status is unconfirmed.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Response</h2>
    <p>
      ZIP results use Census 2020 land overlap. City results use Census place and county overlap, with a representative point where overlap data is unavailable.
      The <code>place</code> object gives the city or county page. If county answers differ, <code>answer</code> is <code>depends</code>.
      Same-state duplicate names normally select the larger place. Use a page slug to select a different place.
    </p>
    <p>
      If a ZIP is absent from the Census data, the response identifies the numeric substitute in <code>matchedZip</code> and explains the match in
      <code>approximationReason</code>. Approximate ZIP and IP results require county confirmation before a status lookup.
      For confirmed reports, read <code>scope</code>, <code>orders</code>, <code>checked</code> and <code>validUntil</code> when available.
    </p>
    <pre><code>{sample}</code></pre>

    <h2>Published active reports</h2>
    <pre><code>curl "{DOCS_ORIGIN}/api/v1/bans"
curl "{DOCS_ORIGIN}/api/v1/bans?state=TX"</code></pre>
    <p>Includes published county bans, scoped orders and other restrictions. Use <code>state</code> to narrow the report.</p>

    <h2>Coverage</h2>
    <pre><code>curl "{DOCS_ORIGIN}/api/v1/coverage"</code></pre>
    <p>Shows directory coverage and which sources are full, positive-only or manual, along with their latest checks and configured cadence.</p>

    <h2>States with county-reporting feeds</h2>
    <ul class="inline-list">
      {live.map((s) => <li><a href={statePath(s.abbr)}>{s.name}</a></li>)}
    </ul>
    <p>
      {restrictions.length} areas have selected sources for local orders, permits or other restrictions. Iowa publishes active proclamations only.
      See <a href="/us/">coverage by state</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Terms</h2>
    <p>
      Confirm with the issuing authority before burning. Verify the publisher's terms before republishing source records.
      The <a href="/data/">dataset page</a> explains how to attribute reports. The <a href="/terms/">terms</a> and <a href="/privacy/">privacy policy</a> cover use, lookup caching and subscriptions.
    </p>
  </main>
</Base>
